<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:controls="using:Telegram.Controls">

    <!--
        TOUCH TARGETS - prepared, not applied.

        Touch mode (AppearanceSettings.TouchMode) does two things on its own: one column
        (MasterDetailPanel.ForceMinimal) and a larger interface scale (InterfaceScale). Those two
        already carry most of the way, because raising the scale to 150% grows every existing
        target by half without a single style change - a 64 px chat-list row measures 96 physical
        px, well past any thumb.

        What scale cannot fix is the handful of controls that are small *in proportion*: a 32 px
        icon button next to a 64 px row is still a 32 px button after everything grows. Those are
        what this file is for.

        Every style here is KEYED, nothing consumes them, AND THIS DICTIONARY IS NOT MERGED. That
        is deliberate on all three counts: applying a container style to the chat list, the message
        list or the header changes the layout of the page the port has just got working, and none of
        it can be judged without a finger on the panel.

        TO APPLY THEM, in this order:
          1. Merge this file in Telegram/App.xaml, LAST in MergedDictionaries:
                 <not_win:ResourceDictionary Source="ms-appx:///Hubs/TouchResources.xaml" />
             Last, because the BasedOn keys below come from dictionaries merged before it -
             DefaultGlyphButtonStyle from Common/CommonStyles.xaml and SettingsCheckBoxStyle from
             Themes/CheckBox_themeresources.xaml. A StaticResource in a merged dictionary can only
             see the dictionaries merged ahead of it, and one that resolves to nothing takes the
             whole application's resources down at startup.
          2. Point the call sites at them, or swap the container style in code when TouchMode is on.

        Note what is NOT based on anything here: the chat list container. Its shipping style,
        ChatListListViewItemStyle, lives in Themes/Generic.xaml, which is the implicit theme
        dictionary and is NOT in the application's lookup chain - a BasedOn pointing at it would
        resolve while Generic.xaml is being parsed and nowhere else. Whoever applies that one has to
        base it on the container style the list is already using, at the list.

        THE NUMBERS. Every touch guideline lands in the same place once its unit is converted:
        Material 48 dp, Fluent 40 px with 44 px recommended, Apple HIG 44 pt - all of them are the
        ~9-10 mm a fingertip covers. At the 96 dpi a XAML DIP is defined at, 10 mm is 37.8 DIPs, so
        48 DIPs is that with margin. This file uses:

          TouchTargetSize     48   the minimum for anything tappable
          TouchTargetLarge    56   for the primary action of a view (send, new chat)
          TouchRowHeight      72   list rows: 64 is Unigram's own, 72 is Telegram for Android's
          TouchGutter         12   the space between two adjacent targets, so a miss lands nowhere
                                   rather than on the wrong one

        These are DIPs, so they are multiplied by the interface scale like everything else - the
        scale is the coarse adjustment and these are the shape.
    -->

    <x:Double x:Key="TouchTargetSize">48</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="TouchTargetLarge">56</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="TouchRowHeight">72</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="TouchGutter">12</x:Double>

    <!--
        Chat list rows: the two setters that have to change, and nothing else.

        These are meant to be COPIED onto the container style the chat list already uses, not
        applied as they stand - a ListViewItem given a Style with no Template has no template at
        all, and the shipping ChatListListViewItemStyle (Themes/Generic.xaml) cannot be reached
        with BasedOn from here. See the header.

        64 -> 72 is Unigram's row height going to Telegram for Android's, and the padding gains the
        4 px of vertical breathing room the taller row leaves over.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TouchChatListRowMetrics"
           TargetType="ListViewItem">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchRowHeight}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding"
                Value="12,4" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Any other list of rows: search results, contacts, members, the folder list. Same height, no
        assumption about the template it is based on.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TouchListViewItemMetrics"
           TargetType="ListViewItem">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchRowHeight}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment"
                Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment"
                Value="Stretch" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The icon buttons: back, search, attach, emoji, the header's overflow. These are the ones
        that stay small when everything else grows, because their size comes from the glyph.
        The glyph itself is left alone - a bigger hit area, not a bigger drawing.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TouchGlyphButtonStyle"
           TargetType="controls:GlyphButton"
           BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultGlyphButtonStyle}">
        <Setter Property="MinWidth"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchTargetSize}" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchTargetSize}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The one button a view is built around: send, or the new-chat button. Large, and with a
        gutter so a thumb aiming at it cannot land on its neighbour.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TouchPrimaryGlyphButtonStyle"
           TargetType="controls:GlyphButton"
           BasedOn="{StaticResource TouchGlyphButtonStyle}">
        <Setter Property="MinWidth"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchTargetLarge}" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchTargetLarge}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin"
                Value="6" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Menu entries. A context menu is where a finger misses most: the entries are the smallest
        targets in the app and they are stacked with no gap at all.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TouchMenuFlyoutItemMetrics"
           TargetType="MenuFlyoutItem">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchTargetSize}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding"
                Value="14,0" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="TouchToggleMenuFlyoutItemMetrics"
           TargetType="ToggleMenuFlyoutItem">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchTargetSize}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding"
                Value="14,0" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Settings rows. They are already 46-48 px tall, so this is mostly about the gutter between
        two rows of a HeaderedControl, which today is zero.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TouchSettingsCheckBoxStyle"
           TargetType="CheckBox"
           BasedOn="{StaticResource SettingsCheckBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight"
                Value="{StaticResource TouchTargetSize}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
